{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-07-sum-difference-product-ratio-puzzles",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Ratio Puzzles with Sums, Differences, and Products",
    "description": "Use ratio parts plus one extra condition to recover actual numbers quickly.",
    "teachingApproach": "Keep one mental model throughout: if numbers are m:n, call them mk and nk. Each new condition—sum, difference, product, average, squares, or LCM—only determines k.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach the common multiplier model: numbers in ratio m:n are mk and nk. Determine k from a known sum, difference, product, average, difference of squares, sum of squares, or LCM when the ratio terms are coprime. Extend to three-number patterns and simple cost/count stories. Learners should choose the shortest condition, solve for k, and then answer only the requested quantity."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio gives the shape of two or three numbers. A second fact gives the scale.\n\nWe’ll use sums, differences, products, squares, averages, and LCM to reveal that missing scale quickly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two numbers in ratio 3:5 can be written 3k and 5k.\n\nIf their sum is 64, then 8k=64, so k=8.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The numbers are 24 and 40.\n\nThe ratio is the shape. The sum tells you how large the shape is.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
